    deeperDive: [
      { q: 'Is this just repeating verses 2-7?', a: 'No — it\'s a concise SYLLOGISM that crystallizes the logical chain. V.2: Self-causation fails. V.7: Other-causation depends on self-caused agents. V.8 combines them into a tight deductive argument. Each verse added something; v.8 synthesizes.', realLifeExample: 'A lawyer\'s closing argument isn\'t repetition — it synthesizes the evidence into a compelling conclusion. V.8 is the closing argument.' },
      { q: 'Why is showing the logical connection important?', a: 'It proves the four options aren\'t truly independent. If they were independent, refuting one wouldn\'t affect the others. But showing that other-causation DEPENDS on self-causation means refuting one automatically refutes the other. The argument is stronger than refuting each separately.', realLifeExample: 'Proving that a building\'s foundation is cracked is more devastating than pointing out individual loose bricks. The foundation crack undermines everything above it.' },
      { q: 'What is the formal logical structure?', a: 'P1: ¬(self-causation) — nothing causes itself. P2: For X to cause Y as "other," X must be established. P3: X\'s establishment requires self-causation (since other-causation leads to regress). P4: But self-causation is impossible (P1). C: Therefore, other-causation is impossible.', realLifeExample: 'P1: No one can certify themselves as a doctor. P2: To practice medicine, you need certification. P3: The certifier needs certification. P4: But self-certification is impossible. C: Therefore, no one can be inherently certified.' },
      { q: 'How does this prepare for v.9?', a: 'V.8 establishes that both self and other causation fail. V.9 will address the remaining options: "both" (which inherits both failures) and "causeless" (which contradicts dependent origination). With v.8\'s synthesis, the elimination of all four options becomes straightforward.', realLifeExample: 'Once you\'ve proven that neither the prosecution\'s nor the defense\'s case holds, addressing the remaining possibilities (compromise verdict, mistrial) becomes much simpler.' },
      { q: 'Is this argument valid by Western logical standards?', a: 'Yes — it\'s a valid hypothetical syllogism (chain argument). If A implies B, and B is impossible, then A is impossible (modus tollens). The structure is logically rigorous by any standard, not just Indian logic.', realLifeExample: 'If "having a driver\'s license" requires "passing the test," and "passing the test" is impossible without studying, and "studying" requires books that don\'t exist — the chain proves you can\'t get a license.' },
      { q: 'What does this tell us about the nature of explanation?', a: 'It suggests that inherent explanation (tracing something to an inherent first cause) is impossible. All explanation is conventional and contextual — we explain things by relating them to other things, not by grounding them in inherent causes.', deeper: 'This has implications for philosophy of science: scientific explanation works by relating phenomena to laws and conditions (conventional explanation), not by grounding them in inherent "first causes." Nāgārjuna\'s critique of inherent causation anticipates the shift from metaphysical to functional explanation in modern philosophy of science.' },
    ],
